----------------------------------------------------------------\
3. HOW IMAGES WORK\
----------------------------------------------------------------\
HTML files do not contain images \'97 they point to image files by\
name. The name in the code must match the file EXACTLY, including\
the extension's letter case (.jpg, .jpeg, .JPG, .png are all\
treated as DIFFERENT on a real web server).\
\
If an image is missing, the page shows a fallback (initials for\
members, a placeholder icon for the gallery) instead of breaking.\
\
Member/PI photos: circular (members) or rounded-square (PI page),\
auto-cropped to center. If a face looks off-center in its circle,\
the simplest fix is to crop that photo file to a square around the\
face (head + shoulders) and re-save with the same name.\
\
----------------------------------------------------------------\
4. GALLERY PHOTO NAMING (photos/ folder)\
----------------------------------------------------------------\
Each event = one "frame" in the gallery. Multiple photos per event\
are supported via a lightbox (click the cover to page through).\
\
Naming: <event-base>-<number>.<ext>\
  e.g. earnshaw-symposium-2023-1.JPG  (the -1 image is the COVER)\
       earnshaw-symposium-2023-2.JPG\
       ...\
\
In photos.html, each event is one line like:\
  <div class="shot" data-base="EVENT-NAME" data-count="N"\
       data-ext="jpg" data-caption="Caption text" data-year="2025"></div>\
\
NOTE (added Sep 2026): the gallery now finds a photo even if the\
extension differs from data-ext \'97 it tries jpg, jpeg, JPG, JPEG,\
png, PNG, webp in turn. data-ext is simply tried first, so keeping\
it correct just makes loading faster. A frame whose photos are not\
uploaded yet shows a neutral placeholder and starts displaying the\
photos automatically as soon as the files land in photos/.\
\
To change an event:\
  - data-count = how many photos that event has\
  - data-ext   = the file extension (jpg / jpeg / JPG / png), all\
                 photos in one event must share the same extension\
  - data-caption / data-year = the label shown\
\
